      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. The dose looks unsafe, and I'd like it confirmed first.\n\n- **Dose:** 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum for metformin is 2,550 mg/day (some labeling allows up to 3,000 mg/day), and a single dose is typically capped at about 1,000 mg. This order is roughly twice the maximum daily dose. It would carry a serious risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Prescriber:** You've said you aren't a clinician. Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, such as the treating physician or pharmacist.\n\n**Next steps**\n1. If this is a typo, tell me the intended dose. For example, 850 mg three times daily is 2,550 mg/day, and 500 mg or 1000 mg twice daily are also common.\n2. If the dose is intentional, the treating prescriber should confirm it and enter or approve the order themselves.\n\nOnce I have a confirmed, safe dose from an appropriate prescriber, I can enter it right away."
      }
